add('real-analysis','completeness','Completeness & sequences','Completeness guarantees that approximations whose internal error vanishes have a limit in the space.',
 '∀ε>0 ∃N ∀m,n≥N: |xₘ−xₙ|<ε  (Cauchy)', ['calculus/limits','foundations/proof'],
 [('Supremum','The least upper bound of a set. It need not belong to the set.'),('Cauchy sequence','A sequence whose sufficiently late terms are arbitrarily close to one another, as in the display.'),('Complete metric space','A metric space in which every Cauchy sequence converges to a point of that space.')],
 [('Completeness of ℝ','Every nonempty subset of ℝ bounded above has a real supremum. Equivalently, every real Cauchy sequence converges in ℝ.'),('Monotone convergence for sequences','Every bounded monotone real sequence converges. An increasing bounded sequence converges to the supremum of its values.'),('Bolzano–Weierstrass','Every bounded sequence in ℝⁿ has a convergent subsequence.')],
 [('Existence proofs','Define a candidate solution as a limit and use completeness to ensure it exists.'),('Iterative computation','Proving an iteration is Cauchy can establish convergence without already knowing its limit.')],
 ['Bounded sequences need not converge: (−1)ⁿ oscillates.','Completeness depends on the space. Rational approximations to √2 can be Cauchy in ℚ without a rational limit.'],
 [('Compute','Find sup{1−1/n:n≥1}. Is it attained?','Every element is below 1 but can get arbitrarily close.','The supremum is 1. It is not attained because 1−1/n<1 for every positive integer n.'),('Prove','Why is every convergent real sequence Cauchy?','Insert the limit between two late terms.','If xₙ→L, choose N so |xₙ−L|<ε/2 for n≥N. Then |xₘ−xₙ|≤|xₘ−L|+|xₙ−L|<ε for m,n≥N.')], ['real-analysis/compactness','real-analysis/function-series'])

add('real-analysis','compactness','Metric compactness & fixed points','Compactness controls arbitrary covers; contraction controls successive approximations.',
 'd(Tx,Ty) ≤ q d(x,y),  0≤q<1', ['real-analysis/completeness','calculus/limits'],
 [('Metric','A nonnegative distance d with d(x,y)=0 iff x=y, symmetry, and the triangle inequality.'),('Compactness','Every open cover has a finite subcover. In metric spaces, this is equivalent to every sequence having a convergent subsequence with limit in the space.'),('Contraction','A self-map T satisfying the displayed bound with one fixed q<1 for all pairs.')],
 [('Heine–Borel','A subset of ℝⁿ with its usual metric is compact exactly when it is closed and bounded.'),('Continuous images','The continuous image of a compact space is compact. A continuous real-valued function on a nonempty compact space attains extrema.'),('Banach fixed-point theorem','A contraction on a nonempty complete metric space has a unique fixed point. Starting anywhere, xₙ₊₁=T(xₙ) converges to it, with d(xₙ,x*)≤qⁿd(x₀,x₁)/(1−q).')],
 [('Numerical equations','A contraction gives existence, uniqueness, an iteration, and an explicit error bound.'),('Uniform control','Compactness often turns local continuity statements into uniform estimates.')],
 ['Closed and bounded does not imply compact in every metric space.','A map with Lipschitz constant 1 need not have a fixed point or a unique one.'],
 [('Compute','For T(x)=(x+2)/3 on ℝ, find its fixed point and contraction constant.','Solve x=T(x) and compare Tx−Ty.','The fixed point is 1. Since |Tx−Ty|=|x−y|/3, q=1/3.'),('Counterexample','Give a continuous function on (0,1) whose image is unbounded.','The domain is not compact.','f(x)=1/x is continuous and unbounded. The compact-image theorem cannot apply to (0,1).')], ['topology/spaces','measure-theory/convergence'])

add('real-analysis','function-series','Series & uniform convergence','A pointwise limit answers one input at a time; a uniform limit controls all inputs at once.',
 'fₙ→f uniformly ⇔ supₓ |fₙ(x)−f(x)|→0', ['real-analysis/completeness','calculus/integrals'],
 [('Series','Σaₙ converges when its partial sums converge. Absolute convergence means Σ|aₙ|<∞.'),('Pointwise convergence','For each fixed x, fₙ(x) tends to f(x); how late n must be may depend on x.'),('Uniform convergence','For every ε>0 a single N makes |fₙ(x)−f(x)|<ε for all x in the domain and all n≥N.')],
 [('Uniform limit theorem','A uniform limit of continuous real- or complex-valued functions is continuous.'),('Weierstrass M-test','If |fₙ(x)|≤Mₙ for all x and ΣMₙ<∞, then Σfₙ converges absolutely at every x and uniformly on the domain.'),('Integration under uniform limits','If Riemann-integrable fₙ on [a,b] converge uniformly to f, then f is Riemann integrable and ∫fₙ→∫f.')],
 [('Approximation','Uniform error bounds justify replacing a function throughout an interval.'),('Power series','Inside a power series radius of convergence, compact subintervals admit strong uniform control.')],
 ['Pointwise convergence does not preserve continuity.','Uniform convergence of functions alone does not justify convergence of their derivatives.'],
 [('Counterexample','For fₙ(x)=xⁿ on [0,1], find the pointwise limit and prove convergence is not uniform.','What happens at 1, and what does a uniform limit preserve?','The limit is 0 for x<1 and 1 at x=1. It is discontinuous, whereas each fₙ is continuous; a uniform limit would be continuous. Also supₓ∈[0,1]|fₙ(x)−f(x)|=1 for every n.'),('Prove','Show Σₙ₌₁∞ xⁿ/n² converges uniformly on [−1,1].','Bound each term independently of x.','|xⁿ/n²|≤1/n² and Σ1/n² converges. The M-test gives uniform convergence.')], ['measure-theory/convergence','complex-analysis/holomorphic'])

add('measure-theory','measurable-spaces','σ-algebras & measures','Not every subset needs to be measurable; specify the collection on which size is defined.',
 'μ(⋃ₙ Aₙ) = Σₙ μ(Aₙ)  for pairwise disjoint measurable Aₙ', ['foundations/sets-maps','real-analysis/completeness'],
 [('σ-algebra','A family ℱ of subsets of X containing X and closed under complements and countable unions. It is consequently closed under countable intersections.'),('Measure','A map μ:ℱ→[0,∞] with μ(∅)=0 and countable additivity on disjoint measurable sets.'),('Borel and Lebesgue sets','The Borel σ-algebra is generated by open subsets. Lebesgue measure on ℝ extends interval length and is complete: all subsets of null sets are measurable.')],
 [('Continuity from below','For increasing measurable Aₙ, μ(⋃Aₙ)=lim μ(Aₙ), allowing +∞.'),('Continuity from above','For decreasing measurable Aₙ with μ(A₁)<∞, μ(⋂Aₙ)=lim μ(Aₙ).'),('Countable null unions','A countable union of measurable sets of measure zero has measure zero.')],
 [('Probability','A probability measure is a measure with total mass 1.'),('Almost-everywhere reasoning','Ignore changes on null sets when studying integration and many function-space properties.')],
 ['A σ-algebra is closed under countable unions, not necessarily arbitrary unions.','The finite-measure assumption for continuity from above cannot simply be omitted.'],
 [('Compute','What is the Lebesgue measure of ℚ∩[0,1]?','Enumerate the rationals and use singleton measures.','Each singleton has measure 0 and the rationals are countable. Countable subadditivity gives total measure 0.'),('Counterexample','Why is finite measure needed for continuity from above?','Consider Aₙ=[n,∞) in ℝ.','The sets decrease and their intersection is empty, of measure 0. Every Aₙ has infinite measure, so the limit of the measures is ∞ rather than 0.')], ['measure-theory/integration','probability/conditioning'])

add('measure-theory','integration','Lebesgue integration & Lᵖ spaces','Build integrals from measurable simple functions, then identify functions that differ only on null sets.',
 '‖f‖ₚ = (∫ |f|ᵖ dμ)¹ᐟᵖ,  1≤p<∞', ['measure-theory/measurable-spaces','calculus/integrals'],
 [('Measurable function','For a real-valued f on (X,ℱ), preimages of Borel subsets of ℝ lie in ℱ.'),('Nonnegative integral','For f≥0 measurable, ∫f is the supremum of integrals of nonnegative simple functions s≤f. For integrable signed f, subtract the integrals of its positive and negative parts.'),('Lᵖ space','For 1≤p<∞, measurable functions with ∫|f|ᵖ<∞, modulo equality almost everywhere. L∞ uses the essential supremum norm.')],
 [('Hölder inequality','If 1/p+1/q=1 with 1≤p,q≤∞, then ∫|fg|≤‖f‖ₚ‖g‖q for f∈Lᵖ and g∈Lq.'),('Minkowski inequality','For 1≤p≤∞, ‖f+g‖ₚ≤‖f‖ₚ+‖g‖ₚ.'),('Completeness','Lᵖ is a Banach space for 1≤p≤∞. L² is a Hilbert space with inner product given by integration of f times the complex conjugate of g.')],
 [('Expectation','E[X] is the integral of a random variable against its probability measure.'),('Function approximation','L² error measures average squared discrepancy and supports orthogonal projection methods.')],
 ['The integral of a signed function is not defined by ∞−∞.','Lᵖ elements are equivalence classes: evaluation at one point is generally not well-defined.'],
 [('Compute','For which finite p≥1 does f(x)=x⁻¹ᐟ² belong to Lᵖ(0,1) with Lebesgue measure?','Integrate x⁻ᵖᐟ² near zero.','The integral is finite exactly when p/2<1. Thus f∈Lᵖ for 1≤p<2, but not for p≥2.'),('Explain','Are 1ℚ and 0 the same element of L²([0,1])?','They differ only on a null set.','Yes. The rationals have Lebesgue measure zero, so 1ℚ=0 almost everywhere and their L² distance is zero.')], ['linear-algebra/orthogonality','measure-theory/convergence'])

add('measure-theory','convergence','Convergence theorems & iterated integrals','Before exchanging a limit or order of integration, identify the theorem whose hypotheses apply.',
 '|fₙ|≤g∈L¹, fₙ→f a.e. ⇒ ∫fₙ→∫f', ['measure-theory/integration','real-analysis/function-series'],
 [('Almost-everywhere convergence','Convergence at all points except a measurable null set.'),('L¹ convergence','∫|fₙ−f|→0. It implies convergence of the integrals and convergence in measure.'),('σ-finiteness','The space is a countable union of measurable sets of finite measure, a standard hypothesis for product-measure results.')],
 [('Monotone convergence','If measurable 0≤fₙ↑f pointwise, then ∫fₙ↑∫f, allowing +∞.'),('Fatou lemma','For nonnegative measurable fₙ, ∫liminf fₙ≤liminf∫fₙ.'),('Dominated convergence','If measurable fₙ→f almost everywhere and |fₙ|≤g almost everywhere for one integrable g, then f is integrable and fₙ→f in L¹.'),('Tonelli and Fubini','On σ-finite measure spaces, a nonnegative measurable function on the product has equal iterated and product integrals, possibly infinite (Tonelli). For an absolutely integrable function the same holds with finite values and almost-everywhere-defined sections (Fubini).')],
 [('Limits of expectations','An integrable envelope permits passing to the limit under expectation.'),('Multidimensional integration','Nonnegativity or absolute integrability justifies integrating one coordinate at a time.')],
 ['Pointwise or almost-everywhere convergence alone does not imply convergence of integrals.','The dominating function must work for every n and be integrable.'],
 [('Diagnose','On (0,1), let fₙ=n·1(0,1/n). Find its pointwise limit and integrals.','The spike becomes taller as its support shrinks.','For every fixed x>0, fₙ(x) eventually vanishes, so the limit is zero. But ∫fₙ=1. There is no common integrable dominator; dominated convergence would otherwise force the integrals to tend to zero.'),('Apply','Show ∫₀¹ xⁿ/(1+x) dx→0.','Ignore the endpoint x=1, a null set, and find a bound.','The integrand tends to zero almost everywhere and is bounded by 1 on [0,1]. Dominated convergence gives limit 0.')], ['probability/limits','real-analysis/function-series'])

add('complex-analysis','holomorphic','Holomorphic functions & power series','Complex differentiability in a neighborhood is far more rigid than real differentiability.',
 'uₓ=vᵧ,  uᵧ=−vₓ  for f=u+iv', ['calculus/multivariable','real-analysis/function-series'],
 [('Complex derivative','f′(z)=limₕ→₀ [f(z+h)−f(z)]/h, with complex h approaching zero from every direction.'),('Holomorphic','Complex differentiable at every point of an open set. Entire means holomorphic on all of ℂ.'),('Analytic','Locally representable by a convergent power series. For complex functions, analyticity and holomorphicity are equivalent.')],
 [('Cauchy–Riemann criterion','If u,v have continuous first partial derivatives on an open set, the displayed equations hold throughout that set exactly when f=u+iv is holomorphic there.'),('Identity theorem','Holomorphic functions on a connected open set that agree on a set with an accumulation point inside the domain agree everywhere.'),('Liouville theorem','A bounded entire function is constant.')],
 [('Rigidity and continuation','Knowing a holomorphic function on a small accumulating set can determine it on a connected domain.'),('Conformal maps','A holomorphic map with nonzero derivative preserves oriented angles locally.')],
 ['Cauchy–Riemann equations at a single point do not alone imply holomorphicity in a neighborhood.','A nonconstant entire function can be bounded on a line without being bounded on the plane.'],
 [('Check','Where is f(z)=the complex conjugate of z complex differentiable?','Write u=x and v=−y.','Nowhere. uₓ=1 but vᵧ=−1, so the necessary Cauchy–Riemann equation fails at every point.'),('Apply','An entire f satisfies |f(z)|≤7 for all z. What can you conclude?','Use Liouville.','f is constant. The bound does not specify which constant, only that its modulus is at most 7.')], ['complex-analysis/contours','complex-analysis/residues'])

add('complex-analysis','contours','Contour integration & Cauchy formulas','Contour integrals connect local values of a holomorphic function to its behavior around a boundary.',
 'f(a) = (1/(2πi)) ∮γ f(z)/(z−a) dz', ['complex-analysis/holomorphic','calculus/integrals'],
 [('Contour integral','For a piecewise C¹ parametrization γ:[a,b]→ℂ, ∫γ f(z)dz=∫ₐᵇ f(γ(t))γ′(t)dt.'),('Primitive','A holomorphic F with F′=f. Then contour integrals depend only on endpoints.'),('Winding number','For a closed contour avoiding a, Ind(γ,a)=(1/(2πi))∮γ dz/(z−a), an integer counting oriented turns.')],
 [('Cauchy integral theorem','A holomorphic function on a simply connected open subset of ℂ has integral zero around every closed piecewise C¹ contour in that domain.'),('Cauchy integral formula','If f is holomorphic on a neighborhood of a closed disk, γ is its positively oriented boundary, and a is inside, the displayed formula holds.'),('Derivative formula','Under the same disk hypotheses, f⁽ⁿ⁾(a)=n!/(2πi) ∮γ f(z)/(z−a)ⁿ⁺¹ dz.')],
 [('Exact evaluation','Extract function values and derivatives from integrals without finding a real-variable antiderivative.'),('Bounds','Cauchy estimates control derivatives using bounds on a surrounding circle.')],
 ['A hole containing a singularity prevents an automatic appeal to simple connectedness.','Orientation matters: reversing a contour negates its integral.'],
 [('Compute','Evaluate ∮|z|=2 eᶻ/z dz counterclockwise.','Use the formula at a=0.','The integral is 2πi e⁰=2πi.'),('Compute','Evaluate ∮|z|=1 dz/z directly with z=eⁱᵗ.','Use dz=i eⁱᵗdt, 0≤t≤2π.','The integrand becomes i dt, giving 2πi. Although 1/z is holomorphic on the punctured plane, that domain is not simply connected.')], ['topology/fundamental-group','complex-analysis/residues'])

add('complex-analysis','residues','Laurent series, poles & residues','The coefficient of (z−a)⁻¹ records the local contribution to a contour integral.',
 '∮γ f(z) dz = 2πi Σa Res(f,a)  (positive simple boundary)', ['complex-analysis/contours'],
 [('Laurent expansion','On an annulus of holomorphicity about a, f has a unique convergent expansion Σₙ∈ℤ cₙ(z−a)ⁿ.'),('Isolated singularity','Removable if all negative Laurent coefficients vanish; a pole if finitely many are nonzero; essential if infinitely many are nonzero.'),('Residue','Res(f,a)=c₋₁. At a simple pole, it is limz→a (z−a)f(z).')],
 [('Residue theorem','If f is holomorphic on a neighborhood of a positively oriented piecewise smooth simple closed contour and its interior except for finitely many isolated singularities inside, its integral is 2πi times their residue sum.'),('Simple quotient pole','If g,h are holomorphic near a, h(a)=0, h′(a)≠0, and g(a)≠0, then Res(g/h,a)=g(a)/h′(a).'),('Removable singularity theorem','A holomorphic function on a punctured neighborhood of a that is bounded near a extends holomorphically across a.')],
 [('Real integrals','Suitable complex contours can evaluate real integrals; estimates must justify any arc contributions being discarded.'),('Coefficient extraction','Contour integrals recover power-series coefficients and support generating-function methods.')],
 ['The pole order is not itself the residue.','A singularity on the integration contour requires separate treatment; the standard residue theorem does not apply directly.'],
 [('Compute','Find Res(1/(z²+1),i).','The denominator derivative is 2z.','The simple-pole formula gives 1/(2i).'),('Classify','Classify the singularity of sin(z)/z at 0 and give its residue.','Use the power series for sine.','sin(z)/z=1−z²/3!+⋯. The singularity is removable with extension value 1; there is no z⁻¹ term, so the residue is 0.')], ['complex-analysis/holomorphic','calculus/integrals'])

add('topology','spaces','Topological spaces & homotopy','Study properties preserved by continuous deformation rather than by a particular distance.',
 'H(x,0)=f(x),  H(x,1)=g(x)', ['foundations/sets-maps','calculus/limits'],
 [('Topology','A family of open subsets containing ∅ and X, closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections.'),('Continuous map and homeomorphism','A map is continuous when preimages of open sets are open. A homeomorphism is a continuous bijection whose inverse is continuous.'),('Homotopy','A continuous H:X×[0,1]→Y interpolating between f and g as displayed. A homotopy equivalence has a continuous inverse up to homotopy, not necessarily an actual inverse.')],
 [('Compact-to-Hausdorff criterion','A continuous bijection from a compact space to a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism.'),('Path connected implies connected','If any two points can be joined by a continuous path, the space cannot be split into two disjoint nonempty open subsets. The converse fails in general.'),('Deformation retraction','If X deformation retracts onto A, then the inclusion A→X is a homotopy equivalence.')],
 [('Shape simplification','Replace a complicated space by a homotopy-equivalent simpler one before computing invariants.'),('Data and geometry','Separate properties of connectivity and holes from the choice of coordinates or scale.')],
 ['A continuous bijection need not have a continuous inverse without additional hypotheses.','Homotopy equivalence is weaker than homeomorphism: a disk and a point are homotopy equivalent, not homeomorphic.'],
 [('Construct','Give a deformation retraction of ℝ² without the origin onto the unit circle.','Move each point along its radial line.','H(x,t)=((1−t)+t/‖x‖)x for x≠0. The coefficient is positive, the endpoint lies on the circle, and circle points stay fixed.'),('Explain','Why is a nonempty convex subset of ℝⁿ contractible?','Choose a point a in it and use straight lines.','H(x,t)=(1−t)x+ta remains in the convex set. It deforms the identity to the constant map at a.')], ['topology/fundamental-group','real-analysis/compactness'])

add('topology','fundamental-group','The fundamental group','Encode the ways a based loop can wind around holes.',
 'π₁(S¹) ≅ ℤ;     π₁(S¹×S¹) ≅ ℤ²', ['topology/spaces','groups/basics'],
 [('Based loop','A continuous path γ:[0,1]→X with γ(0)=γ(1)=x₀.'),('Fundamental group','π₁(X,x₀) consists of loops based at x₀ modulo homotopies fixing both endpoints. Multiplication concatenates loops.'),('Simply connected','A path-connected space with trivial fundamental group. Merely being connected is not enough.')],
 [('Functoriality','A based continuous map f induces a homomorphism f*([γ])=[f∘γ]. Composition and identity maps induce composition and identity homomorphisms.'),('Circle and products','Winding number identifies π₁(S¹) with ℤ. For based spaces X,Y, π₁(X×Y)≅π₁(X)×π₁(Y).'),('Basepoint qualification','In a path-connected space, a path between basepoints induces an isomorphism of their fundamental groups. Different path choices can change it by an inner automorphism.')],
 [('Obstructions','Nonisomorphic fundamental groups rule out homotopy equivalence.'),('Winding','Planar loops around a missing point connect topology to contour integration.')],
 ['The fundamental group is not always abelian: a wedge of two circles has a free group on two generators.','Equal fundamental groups do not imply homeomorphism or homotopy equivalence.'],
 [('Compute','Find π₁(ℝ² without the origin).','Use radial deformation retraction.','The punctured plane deformation retracts onto S¹, so its fundamental group is ℤ.'),('Compute','On a torus, a loop winds twice in the first circle direction and −3 times in the second. What is its class and inverse?','Use π₁(S¹×S¹)≅ℤ².','Its class is (2,−3), and its inverse is (−2,3). Concatenation adds the coordinate pairs.')], ['topology/coverings','knots-braids/knots','category-theory/functors'])

add('topology','coverings','Coverings & van Kampen','Lift loops upstairs, or assemble a fundamental group from overlapping pieces.',
 'π₁(U∪V) ≅ π₁(U) *π₁(U∩V) π₁(V)', ['topology/fundamental-group','groups/quotients'],
 [('Covering map','A continuous map p:E→X such that every x has an open neighborhood U whose preimage is a disjoint union of open sets each mapped homeomorphically onto U.'),('Path lift','A path upstairs mapping to a given path downstairs, with a specified starting point in its fiber.'),('Amalgamated free product','A group formed from two groups by identifying images of a common group. In the display, the maps come from inclusions of the overlap.')],
 [('Path lifting','For a covering map, a path and a starting point above its initial point determine a unique lift.'),('Injectivity on π₁','A covering map induces an injective homomorphism on fundamental groups at corresponding basepoints.'),('Seifert–van Kampen','If U,V are open and path connected, U∩V is nonempty and path connected, and the basepoint lies in the intersection, the displayed amalgamated-product description holds for X=U∪V.')],
 [('Circle covers','The map z↦zⁿ from S¹ to S¹ realizes the subgroup nℤ of ℤ, for n≥1.'),('Computing presentations','Decompose spaces into pieces and translate overlap identifications into generators and relations.')],
 ['A path upstairs need not close even if its projection is a loop.','The basic two-set van Kampen statement needs a path-connected overlap. Disconnected overlaps require a more general version or a different cover.'],
 [('Lift','For p(t)=e²πⁱᵗ from ℝ to S¹, lift γ(s)=e⁴πⁱˢ starting at 0.','Find a real-valued function with the correct exponential.','The unique lift is γ̃(s)=2s. It ends at 2, so the lift is not a loop.'),('Compute','What homomorphism on fundamental groups is induced by z↦z³ on S¹?','Track one counterclockwise turn.','It is ℤ→ℤ, k↦3k. The image is 3ℤ, and the map is injective.')], ['category-theory/universal','knots-braids/braids'])

add('knots-braids','knots','Knots, links & invariants','A knot is an embedding, not just a drawing with crossings.',
 'knot group = π₁(S³ ∖ K)', ['topology/fundamental-group'],
 [('Knot and link','A knot is a smooth (or tame) embedding of S¹ in S³; a link is an embedding of finitely many disjoint circles. Equivalence here is ambient isotopy.'),('Diagram','A generic planar projection with over/under information at crossings.'),('Invariant','A quantity or algebraic object unchanged under the chosen equivalence. Different invariant values distinguish knots; equal values need not identify them.')],
 [('Reidemeister theorem','Two diagrams represent ambient-isotopic tame links exactly when related by planar isotopy and finitely many Reidemeister moves of types I, II, and III.'),('Knot-group invariance','Ambient-isotopic knots have homeomorphic complements and therefore isomorphic fundamental groups.'),('Unknot complement','The fundamental group of the unknot complement in S³ is ℤ; the complement of an open tubular neighborhood is a solid torus.')],
 [('Embedded geometry','Distinguish embeddings of the same abstract circle that cannot be deformed into one another without crossings.'),('Molecular topology','Closed polymer and DNA models use knot and link types to describe entanglement.')],
 ['Changing an overcrossing to an undercrossing is not a Reidemeister move.','The crossing count of a particular diagram is not the minimum crossing number of its knot type.'],
 [('Explain','Does adding one removable curl to an unknot diagram change its knot type?','Identify the Reidemeister move.','No. A type-I move adds or removes such a curl by ambient isotopy. It changes diagram crossing count without changing the knot.'),('Reason','If a knot has a nonabelian knot group, can it be the unknot?','Compare with the unknot group.','No. The unknot group is ℤ, which is abelian. Isomorphism preserves commutativity, so the groups cannot be isomorphic.')], ['knots-braids/braids','groups/quotients'])

add('knots-braids','braids','Braid groups & closures','Record strand motion while retaining information that a final permutation forgets.',
 'σᵢσᵢ₊₁σᵢ = σᵢ₊₁σᵢσᵢ₊₁;  σᵢσⱼ=σⱼσᵢ if |i−j|≥2', ['groups/basics','knots-braids/knots'],
 [('Braid group Bₙ','Isotopy classes of n strands monotone in height, joining fixed top endpoints to the same set of bottom endpoints. Multiplication stacks braids; inverses reverse crossings and stacking order.'),('Artin generator','σᵢ exchanges adjacent strands i and i+1 by a specified positive crossing; σᵢ⁻¹ uses the opposite crossing.'),('Closure','Connect corresponding top and bottom endpoints outside the braid to obtain a link. The endpoint permutation determines the number of closure components via its cycle count.')],
 [('Artin presentation','Bₙ is generated by σ₁,…,σₙ₋₁ with the two displayed families of relations. Unlike in Sₙ, there is no relation σᵢ²=e.'),('Alexander theorem','Every oriented tame link is isotopic to the closure of some braid.'),('Markov theorem','Two braid closures represent the same oriented link type exactly when the braids are connected by conjugation and stabilization or destabilization β↔βσₙ±¹, allowing a change in strand count.')],
 [('Algebraic knot descriptions','Use braid words to describe links and manipulate them algebraically.'),('Motion planning','Braids record collision-free exchanges of points in a plane, including winding missed by a permutation alone.')],
 ['Two braids with the same permutation need not be the same braid.','Equivalent closures need not come from equal or even same-strand-count braids.'],
 [('Compute','In B₂, does σ₁² equal the identity? What is its endpoint permutation?','B₂ has one generator and no defining relation imposing finite order.','No. B₂≅ℤ, with σ₁ corresponding to 1, so σ₁² corresponds to 2. Its endpoint permutation is the identity because the two exchanges cancel in S₂.'),('Compute','How many components does the closure of a braid with endpoint permutation (123)(45) have?','Count cycles, including fixed points if there are any.','There are two cycles, so the closure has two components.')], ['groups/actions','representations/basics','topology/coverings'])

add('category-theory','categories','Categories & morphisms','Describe structure through composable maps, without assuming the objects are sets with elements.',
 '(h∘g)∘f = h∘(g∘f)', ['foundations/sets-maps','groups/basics'],
 [('Category','Objects, morphisms with specified source and target, identity morphisms, and associative composition with the identity laws.'),('Isomorphism','A morphism f:X→Y with a two-sided inverse g:Y→X. The relevant category determines what structure is preserved.'),('Monomorphism and epimorphism','A monomorphism is left-cancellable: f∘g=f∘h implies g=h. An epimorphism is right-cancellable: g∘f=h∘f implies g=h.')],
 [('Inverse uniqueness','A morphism with a two-sided inverse has exactly one such inverse.'),('Isomorphisms cancel','Every isomorphism is both a monomorphism and an epimorphism; the converse need not hold in a general category.'),('Set-specific characterization','In Set, monomorphisms are injections and epimorphisms are surjections. This characterization is not a definition valid in all categories.')],
 [('Shared language','Groups and homomorphisms, spaces and continuous maps, and vector spaces and linear maps each form categories.'),('Orders as categories','A poset defines a category with one arrow x→y when x≤y and no arrow otherwise.')],
 ['A bijection of underlying sets need not be an isomorphism in the structured category.','Composition is only defined when source and target match.'],
 [('Prove','If g and h are both two-sided inverses of f, show g=h.','Insert the identity as f∘h.','g=g∘id=g∘(f∘h)=(g∘f)∘h=id∘h=h.'),('Identify','In the category associated to a poset, when are two objects isomorphic?','An inverse requires arrows in both directions.','Exactly when they are equal: arrows both ways mean x≤y and y≤x, and antisymmetry gives x=y.')], ['category-theory/functors','category-theory/universal'])

add('category-theory','functors','Functors, naturality & Yoneda','Transport structure between categories and compare those transports coherently.',
 'G(f)∘ηX = ηY∘F(f),  f:X→Y', ['category-theory/categories'],
 [('Functor','A map on objects and morphisms preserving sources, targets, identities, and composition. A contravariant functor reverses arrows, equivalently a functor from the opposite category.'),('Natural transformation','For F,G:C→D, a family ηX:F(X)→G(X) satisfying the displayed naturality equation for every morphism f.'),('Representable functor','A covariant set-valued functor is representable if naturally isomorphic to HomC(A,−) for some object A. Assume hom-collections are sets.')],
 [('Yoneda lemma, covariant form','For a locally small category C and F:C→Set, natural transformations HomC(A,−)→F correspond bijectively to elements of F(A), by η↦ηA(idA).'),('Naturality of induced invariants','The fundamental group construction is a functor from based spaces and based continuous maps to groups and homomorphisms.')],
 [('Coordinate-free constructions','Naturality expresses compatibility with every allowed change of object, rather than a lucky choice of coordinates.'),('Recovering objects from maps','Yoneda formalizes how an object is determined, up to isomorphism, by its pattern of maps to or from other objects.')],
 ['A component map for every object is not enough: all naturality squares must commute.','A vector-space basis gives an isomorphism to its dual, but arbitrary basis choices do not make that construction natural.'],
 [('Construct','For a vector space V, define jV:V→V** by jV(v)(ℓ)=ℓ(v). Show compatibility with a linear map T:V→W.','Evaluate T**jV(v) on an arbitrary ℓ∈W*.','(T**jV(v))(ℓ)=jV(v)(ℓ∘T)=ℓ(Tv)=jW(Tv)(ℓ). Hence T**∘jV=jW∘T. In finite dimension jV is an isomorphism.'),('Identify','Under Yoneda, what determines a natural transformation Hom(A,−)→F?','Look at the identity of A.','The element a=ηA(idA)∈F(A). Conversely, for u:A→X, define ηX(u)=F(u)(a); functoriality gives naturality.')], ['topology/fundamental-group','category-theory/universal','linear-algebra/spaces-maps'])

add('category-theory','universal','Universal properties, products & adjunctions','Specify an object by the maps it uniquely permits rather than by one construction.',
 'HomD(FX,Y) ≅ HomC(X,UY), naturally in X and Y', ['category-theory/functors'],
 [('Product','An object P with projections to A and B such that any pair X→A and X→B factors through one unique map X→P.'),('Coproduct','The dual construction: injections from A and B into C such that maps A→X and B→X extend uniquely to C→X.'),('Adjunction','Functors F:C→D and U:D→C with the displayed bijections natural in both variables. F is left adjoint to U.')],
 [('Uniqueness of universal objects','Two products of the same objects are uniquely isomorphic by an isomorphism preserving their projections; analogous statements hold for other universal properties.'),('Free–forgetful adjunction','The free-group functor is left adjoint to the underlying-set functor: a set map X→U(G) extends uniquely to a group homomorphism F(X)→G.'),('Preservation','Right adjoints preserve limits and left adjoints preserve colimits, whenever the relevant limits or colimits exist.')],
 [('Construction transfer','Products, quotients, and free objects can be recognized by the same mapping patterns across different categories.'),('Gluing','Pushouts formalize compatible gluing; van Kampen expresses a fundamental group as a group pushout.')],
 ['A categorical product need not be implemented as a Cartesian product of underlying sets.','An adjunction is not generally an equivalence: its unit and counit need not be isomorphisms.'],
 [('Compare','What are the coproducts of two sets in Set and of two groups in Grp?','Ask which construction accepts arbitrary pairs of outgoing maps.','In Set it is disjoint union. In Grp it is the free product, not generally the direct product, because maps from the two groups need not have commuting images.'),('Prove','Why are two products P and Q of A,B isomorphic?','Use each product property to construct a map to the other.','The projection pairs give unique maps P→Q and Q→P. Each composite preserves the relevant projections, so uniqueness forces it to equal the identity. Thus the maps are inverse.')], ['topology/coverings','groups/quotients','algebra/rings-fields'])

# Bridge sheets connect the probability and analysis branches.
add('probability','convergence','Modes of convergence','Distinguish pathwise behavior, small error probabilities, average error, and convergence of laws.',
 'Lᵖ ⇒ probability ⇒ distribution; almost surely ⇒ probability', ['probability/random-variables','measure-theory/convergence'],
 [('Almost sure convergence','Xₙ→X almost surely means P({ω: Xₙ(ω)→X(ω)})=1. Variables are defined on a common probability space.'),('Convergence in probability and Lᵖ','Convergence in probability means P(|Xₙ−X|>ε)→0 for every ε>0. For 1≤p<∞, Lᵖ convergence means E|Xₙ−X|ᵖ→0.'),('Convergence in distribution','For real random variables, Xₙ converges in distribution to X if their distribution functions converge at every continuity point of the distribution function of X. A common probability space is not required.')],
 [('Implications','Almost sure convergence implies convergence in probability. Lᵖ convergence implies convergence in probability by Markov’s inequality. Convergence in probability implies convergence in distribution.'),('A constant limit','Convergence in distribution to a constant c implies convergence in probability to c.'),('Dominated convergence in Lᵖ','If Xₙ→X almost surely and |Xₙ|≤Y for all n, with E[Yᵖ]<∞, then Xₙ→X in Lᵖ. Apply dominated convergence to |Xₙ−X|ᵖ≤2ᵖYᵖ.')],
 [('Limit theorems','The strong law concerns almost sure convergence of averages; the central limit theorem concerns convergence in distribution of rescaled errors.'),('Interchanging limits and expectations','L¹ convergence guarantees E[Xₙ]→E[X]. Convergence in probability alone does not control rare, large values.')],
 ['Equal distributions do not imply that random variables are close on a given probability space.','Almost sure convergence does not by itself imply convergence of expectations. A domination or uniform integrability condition can supply the missing control.'],
 [('Counterexample','On (0,1) with uniform probability, let Xₙ(ω)=n when ω<1/n and 0 otherwise. Find the almost sure limit and E[Xₙ].','Fix a positive ω first, then compute the area under the spike.','For any ω>0, eventually n>1/ω, so Xₙ(ω)=0. Thus Xₙ→0 almost surely, but E[Xₙ]=n·(1/n)=1. There is no convergence in L¹.'),('Prove','Show Lᵖ convergence implies convergence in probability.','Apply Markov’s inequality to |Xₙ−X|ᵖ.','For ε>0, P(|Xₙ−X|>ε)≤E|Xₙ−X|ᵖ/εᵖ→0. The bound controls each fixed error threshold.'),('Counterexample','Let X take values −1 and 1 equally likely, and set Xₙ=−X for every n. Does Xₙ converge in distribution to X? In probability?','Compare the distributions and then |Xₙ−X|.','Every Xₙ has the same distribution as X, so convergence in distribution holds. But |Xₙ−X|=2 surely, so convergence in probability fails.')],
 ['probability/limits','measure-theory/convergence','statistics/estimation'])

add('probability','conditional-expectation','Conditional expectation','Average using the information available; least squares and conditioning are versions of the same projection idea.',
 'E[E[X | 𝒢]] = E[X]', ['probability/conditioning','measure-theory/integration','linear-algebra/orthogonality'],
 [('Information','A sub-σ-algebra 𝒢⊆ℱ records which events are observable. E[X|Y] abbreviates E[X|σ(Y)].'),('Conditional expectation','For integrable real X, E[X|𝒢] is a 𝒢-measurable integrable random variable Z satisfying ∫ₐZ dP=∫ₐX dP for every A∈𝒢. It exists and is unique up to almost sure equality.'),('Finite partition','If 𝒢 is generated by a finite partition with positive-probability cells Aᵢ, then E[X|𝒢] equals E[X1ₐᵢ]/P(Aᵢ) on each cell.')],
 [('Tower property','If ℋ⊆𝒢⊆ℱ and X is integrable, E[E[X|𝒢]|ℋ]=E[X|ℋ] almost surely. In particular, taking total expectations recovers E[X].'),('Taking out known factors','For bounded 𝒢-measurable Y and integrable X, E[YX|𝒢]=Y E[X|𝒢] almost surely.'),('Least-squares projection','If X∈L², then E[X|𝒢] uniquely minimizes E[(X−Z)²] among 𝒢-measurable Z∈L², up to almost sure equality. The residual is orthogonal to every such Z.')],
 [('Prediction','E[X|Y] is the best squared-error predictor of X from Y. Ordinary linear regression restricts predictions to linear combinations of features; the conditional mean need not be linear.'),('Variance decomposition','For X∈L², Var(X)=E[Var(X|𝒢)]+Var(E[X|𝒢]). This separates uncertainty remaining after observation from variation explained by it.')],
 ['E[X|𝒢] is generally a random variable, not a single number.','The tower property requires nested information. Conditioning on an event of probability zero cannot use the elementary ratio formula.'],
 [('Compute','A fair die gives X. You learn only whether X is odd. Find E[X|parity] and verify its expectation.','Average separately on {1,3,5} and {2,4,6}.','The conditional mean is 3 on odd outcomes and 4 on even outcomes. Its expectation is (3+4)/2=3.5=E[X].'),('Prove','Write m=E[X|𝒢] for X∈L². Show E[(X−Z)²]=E[(X−m)²]+E[(m−Z)²] for any 𝒢-measurable Z∈L².','Expand the square and use residual orthogonality.','Write X−Z=(X−m)+(m−Z). The cross term has expectation zero because m−Z is 𝒢-measurable and X−m is orthogonal to L²(𝒢). The two remaining squares give the identity, proving minimality and uniqueness up to almost sure equality.')],
 ['statistics/regression','probability/convergence','measure-theory/measurable-spaces'])
